WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
 So the first topic that we're going to talk about is confidentiality.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.600
 Encryption and decryption.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:09.920
 And of course, this was what was going on at Bletchley Park in World War II.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:16.040
 So the terminology that we'll use in this is plaintext and ciphertext.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.640
 And the idea is, whether it's text or other information,

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.880
 there is the information that we actually want to transmit,

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.000
 whether it's a credit card number or something else.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.960
 And then there is the encrypted version of that.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.480
 And we'll call that the ciphertext.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.040
 And the ciphertext is what we assume is revealed to intermediate parties,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.540
 whether they are stopping it and changing it or they're just watching it.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:44.520
 It's still the ciphertext is the stuff that we are just by the nature

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.840
 of the communication, we are forced to reveal it.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.400
 Or there is a probability that we'll reveal it.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.960
 So it is hopefully unintelligible.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.600
 And hopefully, it is difficult to go from the ciphertext to the plaintext,

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.840
 except if you are the actual intended recipient, or impossible.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.320
 Encryption is the act of going from plaintext to ciphertext.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.200
 And returning the ciphertext back to the plaintext is decryption.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.800
 And there is a key, some kind of a key which is really

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:19.600
 sort of some data plus a technique, plus an algorithm that goes back and forth.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.680
 So there are two kinds of systems that we'll talk about in the upcoming lectures.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.880
 One is called a secret key, and the other is called a public key.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.080
 The secret key is the one we talked about at the very beginning.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:39.640
 The secret key was really used from the Romans and Caesar on up to World War II.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:44.840
 The public key encryption really is much more recent in the 60s and the 70s.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.160
 And we'll talk about that later on.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:51.840
 So the first thing we'll talk about is the shared secret or secret key.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.080
 The secret key is also called symmetric key, which

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:59.000
 means that both parties have to be in possession of the same information.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:04.560
 You basically use the same key material to encrypt as you do to decrypt.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.400
 The public key is asymmetric, which means you use one key to encrypt

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.800
 and a different key to decrypt.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.360
 We'll get to that later.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:19.440
 And so the problem that secret key has that led to the need to invent public key

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.400
 is the fact that you need to, at some point, have a secure communication.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:26.600
 Whether you're sitting in a room together and you hand each other code books,

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:32.120
 whatever it is, you have to have a way to distribute the key in a secure manner.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.360
 The public key, which we'll get to later, has a way of distributing the key

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.760
 using insecure medium.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.960
 And you'll see when we get there, it's so obvious and clever,

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:47.560
 you wonder why nobody thought of it until very recently.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.840
 So here is the path.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.240
 You have some plain text.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.760
 You have, say, the word candy that you want to send.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.440
 You're going to encrypt with a shift, where you just

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:00.920
 go to the next later letter.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:07.560
 So C becomes D. A becomes B. N becomes O. And so now we have the D-B-O-E-Z.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.520
 That is the plain text coming from Alice.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:18.360
 Alice sends it in the dangerous, dangerous, nasty, wide world of routers

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:25.200
 or radio with Morse code or whatever it is we're going to do.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.080
 Whatever it is we're going to do, where the message might

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:29.640
 be intercepted by somebody like Eve.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.680
 Now, they're not intercepting the plain text.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.680
 We assume that this part here is secure and this part here is secure.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.320
 It's only dangerous while it's in flight somehow in the middle.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.760
 And we only worry about Eve getting it.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:47.640
 And then at some point, because Bob has the key, which is subtract 1,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.920
 Bob goes from each of the cyber text letters

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.200
 back to the plain text letters.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.320
 And voila, out comes the plain text again.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:03.800
 And so Eve's problem is Eve is only handed-- well, no, sorry.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:05.520
 Eve is not given the key.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.160
 Eve is given the cipher text and nothing else.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.960
 And she must, like Bletchley Park, must derive whatever it is.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.960
 Derive the key, derive the plain text, whatever it is, that's Eve's goal.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:26.240
 The Caesar cipher is kind of the oldest, most widely used forms of encryption.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.400
 It uses the notion of a shift.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.680
 The shift number is just as I've shown.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:41.640
 A shift of 1 means a becomes b and x becomes y and l becomes m.

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.640
 So you just take and move a fixed position down the l.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.580
 This was used for a surprisingly long period of time.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.360
 And there's some pretty good YouTube videos

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.260
 that if you want to see more about how this works and the math behind it

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.040
 and how you break it, it's pretty fascinating.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.440
 I mean, finally, it's completely breakable.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:05.160
 And we're actually going to break it here pretty soon ourselves.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.360
 So the Caesar cipher.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.960
 So I want to pause and let you see a YouTube video here

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:18.440
 from a beloved movie called The Christmas Story, where little Ralphie

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.840
 gets his Little Orphan Annie secret decoder ring.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:27.480
 And Little Orphan Annie sends a decoded message, an encoded cipher

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.040
 text, through the radio.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.120
 Everyone can hear it, but only those people who have the secret decoder

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.120
 ring can decrypt the message.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.280
 And you can see that it is a Caesar cipher.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:38.080
 It has a shift.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.440
 You'll note that the first thing they say before they

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:46.120
 say the encrypted message in the radio is that you're

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:49.080
 supposed to connect b and 13 or something like that.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:54.200
 And then you rotate the two wheels of the secret decoder ring to the b 13.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:57.800
 And then you can read across the secret decoder ring

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.280
 and decrypt the message as it's decrypted.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.160
 And then he slowly decrypts it.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.360
 And then, of course, there is the delightful moment

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:12.240
 where he realizes the crash, crass commercialism,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.080
 or the complete lack of interesting meaning in all of this.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.720
 So without further ado, let's take a look at Ralphie and Christmas Story.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:21.600
 We'll be right back.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.400
 So I hope you like that.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.600
 I hope you like that.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.600
 And so off we go.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:35.200
 We're going to have a secret decoder ring for this class.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:39.000
 I would love to be able to send you all--

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:39.760
 come down.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:42.680
 I would love to be able to send you all a little mechanical wheel

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.400
 to move the stuff back and forth.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:48.840
 But instead, I use the internet, and I'm going to send you a PDF.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:55.120
 And at this point, you might want to pause the video and grab this PDF.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:07:01.280
 Grab it, secretdecoder.pdf, drchuck.com/secret-decoder.pdf,

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:02.360
 and download it.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:05.240
 And you might even want to print it out, because we're going to,

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:11.760
 at this moment, do a code breaking exercise.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:15.600
 And so let me tell you how to use this secret decoder ring.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:19.240
 So the top line here is the plain text.

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:24.000
 And if you recall, Caesar Shift has a shift number.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.280
 And so to encode, you go from a plain text.

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:31.440
 Let's say I want to do Chuck.

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.640
 I want to encode Chuck.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.580
 And I want to encode it with a shift of 2.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.400
 So a shift of 2 means we select this.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:46.680
 And we basically go C is our plain text.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:52.840
 And then down, we go E. So then we go E is our first letter.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:57.160
 And then H is our second letter.

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:58.720
 So we go down.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.120
 And that means H becomes J. Yeah.

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:08.760
 And so U becomes W. So now I'm doing my encryption.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:14.520
 So EHW is the encrypted ciphertext.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.560
 So let me clear that and write back down here.

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:24.000
 Let me give me a different color, EHW.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:26.720
 Now to decrypt, remember, you need to know the shift.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:29.440
 So we somehow communicated separately and securely

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.040
 what the shift was.

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:34.120
 And so now we have received our ciphertext.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:38.640
 We received our ciphertext.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.000
 And we need to decrypt it.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:42.920
 And so we know that the shift is 2.

00:08:42.920 --> 00:08:46.640
 So we go to E. We go in the shift row.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.320
 And then we go up to the plain text row.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:52.000
 And that says the first one is C. Then we go to H.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.960
 We go to the encoded text row.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.160
 And we go back up to the plain text row.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.960
 And so that's an H. Oh, wait, wait, wait.

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:03.320
 It's not HW.

00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:04.080
 What am I thinking?

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.480
 This should have been a J. I got this wrong.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.680
 Sorry about that.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.200
 So that's wrong.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.240
 Here's a J. Moves up to the H. Then the W.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.440
 Let's see if I got W right.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:13.940
 Yeah.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.920
 So W is my last ciphertext.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:21.240
 And it goes up to the U. Dot, dot, dot, dot, dot.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:27.280
 So you see the pattern that our encoding is plain text down

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:28.640
 to shift position.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:33.840
 And our decoding is shift position back up to plain text.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.080
 So this is our secret decoder ring.

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.960
 So go grab it and download it so that you can participate

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.920
 in the next exercise.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:50.260
 So now you are going to be cast in the role of Bletchley Park.

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:50.960
 OK?

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:51.680
 Ready?

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:56.480
 So here is your first code-breaking exercise.

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:58.080
 OK.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.600
 So here we go.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.160
 So you're Bletchley Park, right?

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.960
 You just intercepted this ciphertext.

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:12.600
 U-B-- oh, U-P-B-T-U. Whoa, it's encrypted.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:15.000
 It's clearly meaningless.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.800
 So how are you going to decrypt it?

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:21.240
 Well, the technique is take a look

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:24.880
 and decrypt it with all the shift numbers, right?

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.240
 You're going to do this by hand.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.360
 You're going to be a computer yourself.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:32.160
 You're going to do all the shift decrypting.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.440
 And just like in Bletchley Park, you

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:38.220
 know you succeeded when the plain text makes sense, right?

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:40.680
 When the plain text makes no sense,

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:41.840
 then you haven't succeeded.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.480
 But at some point, the plain text makes sense.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.000
 So what you need to do is take your secret decoder ring,

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.440
 and you need to decrypt it with a shift of 1, a shift of 2,

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.720
 a shift of 3, a shift of 4.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:57.640
 And if you have your family members around,

00:10:57.640 --> 00:10:59.120
 you can print out multiple copies

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:00.680
 of the secret decoder ring, and you

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:03.520
 can assign different shifts to different family members.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:08.320
 So you have to decrypt this 26 times.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.000
 You have to decrypt it 26 times.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:12.160
 And then you look at all the 26 decryptions,

00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:17.080
 and then you decide which one makes the most sense, OK?

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:20.160
 So don't peek.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:21.800
 Decrypt this one.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:24.400
 I made it easy on you, OK?

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:27.640
 So we'll stop now and give you a little bit of time

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:29.480
 to decrypt this one.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:32.360
 Don't start pause until you actually have decrypted it,

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:32.860
 OK?

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:44.160
 OK, this is your last chance for spoiler alert.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:44.920
 So here we are.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:47.200
 We're about to decrypt it.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:48.680
 Here we go.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:50.240
 I did make it easy on you.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:51.400
 It was a shift of 1.

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:56.680
 It was a shift of 1.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:59.440
 If you started at 12, you're kind of foolish, right?

00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:02.880
 So you started at 1, and you go like, oh, great.

00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:04.920
 So now I'm going to decrypt it.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:06.320
 I'll start at 1.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:07.120
 Here's the plain text.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:07.960
 This should be PP.

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:09.220
 That's the plain text.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:14.640
 So I'll start with U. If it's 1, then I go up, and it's T.

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:20.820
 And the second one is P. So I go up, and it's O, T-O.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:21.320
 Keep going.

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:23.120
 It says toast.

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:27.760
 So you say to yourself, well, that's a word, so it must be it.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.320
 Well, hello.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.200
 What are you doing here?

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:34.080
 Do you want to say hi to my students?

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:34.580
 Meow.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.320
 This is a cat.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:41.000
 This is an Eddie cat.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.780
 He likes to come up into my office and look.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:49.120
 So do you know anything about encryption?

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:50.520
 Do you know anything about encryption?

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:55.400
 So you use a shift of 1, and then you go from the encryption

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.600
 text up to the plain text.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:12:59.720
 Meow.

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:00.600
 OK.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:04.440
 You are clearly not interested in my lecture.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:06.000
 So that was my cat.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.520
 Hello.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:08.040
 Sorry.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.040
 Can't open the window for you, because I'm doing a lecture.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.640
 OK.

00:13:13.640 --> 00:13:19.040
 So you're going to just keep bugging that window

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.560
 until I kick you out of the other room, aren't you?

00:13:21.560 --> 00:13:23.480
 So you're going to have to get kicked out.

00:13:23.480 --> 00:13:29.200
 Out you go.

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:34.920
 He was going to keep hitting that until I opened it for him.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.200
 OK.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.160
 So now you've broken this code.

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:40.960
 And again, just like in Bletchley Park,

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.280
 you only knew that you broke it if it made sense.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:48.360
 And so luckily, in Bletchley Park, the messages were longer,

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:51.400
 and they were often looking for canonical things

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.240
 that they would say every day.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:55.640
 So here we go.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:14:02.120
 And so that's the breaking of that one.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.760
 And it turns out the shift of 1 was the thing that we did.

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:09.560
 So here is your second task.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:13.880
 This one's longer, and it's not a shift of 1.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:17.560
 And so this is a situation where you really

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:20.080
 would have to get your whole family going on this,

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:23.040
 where you've got to do 26 decryptions of this.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:26.320
 And it will make sense to you.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:29.480
 You decrypt it 26 times.

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:31.960
 And so this one's going to be harder.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.080
 I guess you could just decrypt one word,

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:37.320
 but it's just not a shift of 1.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:40.760
 But now we're going to do another trick.

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:44.040
 So I don't want you to try all 26,

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.200
 because there's a mistake in this.

00:14:46.200 --> 00:14:49.240
 There is a leakage of information

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:51.960
 that makes it so that you can figure out

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.480
 what the right decryption to try might be.

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:57.440
 So this is English.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:00.000
 This is an English sentence.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:04.280
 So stare at it for a while and find a more optimal way

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:08.880
 to decrypt it than trying all 26 shift patterns.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:10.680
 So there's a way to optimize this,

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:16.120
 a way to cleverly figure out what might be the best shift,

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:20.520
 or how not to have to decrypt the entire message 26 times

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:22.760
 to reduce the complexity.

00:15:22.760 --> 00:15:25.720
 And that's because we've leaked some information here that

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:28.160
 should be pretty obvious to you.

00:15:28.160 --> 00:15:30.560
 So let me give you a moment to break this one.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:32.200
 It shouldn't take you too long, and you

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:34.880
 shouldn't have to force your whole family to decrypt

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:36.520
 this stuff.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:37.400
 So here we go.

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:38.840
 Give you a chance to decrypt it.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:49.480
 Here's your last chance before the reveal.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:50.600
 You ready?

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:51.080
 Here we go.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:59.840
 So here is the decrypted text.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:03.120
 The shift turns out to be 13.

00:16:03.120 --> 00:16:05.440
 It's a shift to 13.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:12.240
 And the weakness of this whole thing is this right here.

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:16.440
 In the English language, what is a single character--

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.480
 we're not encrypting the spaces, you'll notice,

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:20.680
 because the spaces come across.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:25.760
 So what is the one single character word

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:31.800
 that we have in the English language that's capitalized?

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:37.160
 Well, that's usually I. I need a jet, money and a jet.

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:42.880
 What is the one thing that we do in the English language that

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:47.840
 is a single character word that is lowercase, typically,

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:49.600
 unless at the beginning of the sentence?

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:52.080
 And that is the letter A.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:55.000
 So basically, you didn't have to decrypt the whole message.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:56.920
 You see some weird pattern.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:16:58.320
 And you go like this.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.280
 I just have to figure out.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.120
 And then you go look.

00:17:02.120 --> 00:17:05.880
 And you look in the row 13, the plain text.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:09.000
 And you go like, oh, where is-- you go look at the I,

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:10.760
 because you guessed to the plain text.

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:13.120
 And then you just look down until you see the V.

00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:15.680
 And within seconds, literally, within seconds,

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.800
 if you do it right, within seconds,

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:19.560
 you know it's a shift to 13.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:24.120
 And then it's a trivial matter to convert it.

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:26.280
 So you could figure out the shift code within seconds.

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:29.720
 And this was how Letchley Park figured it out.

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:34.040
 And this is why the known plain text was so important.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:36.200
 Because you'd only have to figure out like one letter

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:37.960
 if you knew what the plain text was.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:42.000
 And often, they would know by length and certain other things,

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.800
 oh, I think we can guess what this plain text was

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:48.080
 that this particular operator would send.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:50.320
 And this has to do with the leakage of information.

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:53.000
 It's not the mathematical perfection or lack

00:17:53.000 --> 00:17:55.200
 of perfection in the security key.

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:56.520
 It's some other leak.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:17:58.720
 It's some other thing where they're going like,

00:17:58.720 --> 00:17:59.480
 oh, wait a sec.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:02.000
 I can take advantage of something.

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:04.880
 It was just equally encrypted as any other message.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:07.840
 But because I gave you this clue of an uppercase

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:10.920
 single character word and a lowercase single character

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:15.920
 word, lowercase single character word and uppercase single character

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:18.200
 word, I greatly reduced the amount of effort

00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:22.000
 that you had to put in, OK?

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:25.760
 Now what's cool about this-- you can go to this website,

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:30.440
 www.wrote13.com-- is that long ago, before Facebook

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.320
 and before Twitter and before all these things,

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:35.040
 we had these things called newsgroups.

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:38.320
 And they were kind of this weird kind of collective email

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:39.240
 list that we had.

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:42.160
 And this was like in the '80s.

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.360
 And it was even used in store and forward networks,

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:46.880
 where it was kind of like Facebook and store and forward

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:50.000
 networks, meaning that it might take four hours for you

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.000
 to see the status update.

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:55.280
 But we kind of would subscribe to these collective things.

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:59.280
 And there was one that was basically the dirty jokes.

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:02.800
 And the thing about dirty jokes was part

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:05.160
 of what we were trying to do in this thing

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:07.160
 was you weren't supposed to swear.

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:12.600
 And there was software that would filter out swear words

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:19.040
 from-- if you put a swear word in to a dirty joke,

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:20.480
 it would not forward the message.

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:24.180
 And so we had to have a way to encrypt messages that

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:28.280
 included swear words so that we could tell dirty jokes

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:30.240
 to each other for those who wanted to subscribe

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:31.600
 to the dirty joke list.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.840
 And so they came up with this Rote 13.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:36.000
 So we came up with a simple Caesar cipher

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:37.760
 with a shift of 13.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:41.560
 13, beautifully, of course, is 26 divided by 2.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:43.160
 So it's a symmetric shift.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:46.600
 The shifting in by 13 is the same as shifting out.

00:19:46.600 --> 00:19:51.240
 So of all the Caesar ciphers, a shift of 13,

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:52.560
 the encryption and the decryption

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.080
 are exactly the same calculation.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:20:00.600
 And so we would type our dirty joke into Rote 13

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:01.720
 and convert it to Rote 13.

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:04.000
 And we would send it in Rote 13.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:05.920
 And then we would-- if we wanted to decrypt it.

00:20:05.920 --> 00:20:08.440
 But what became funny after a while

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:12.880
 was we were so used to reading Rote 13

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:15.800
 that it almost became like a second language.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:17.320
 We could read second.

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:20.400
 We could start reading the dirty jokes in Rote 13.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.160
 And we would laugh before we translated them out.

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:27.320
 So Rote 13 is an interesting sort of historical thing.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:29.720
 And you go to Rote 13 and sort of encrypt

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:31.000
 whatever you want to say.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:33.040
 And I'll probably have some questions

00:20:33.040 --> 00:20:38.240
 to ask you where you will have to do some Rote 13 encryption.

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:39.720
 And so that's the end of this lecture

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.160
 where we talk about Caesar ciphers

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:45.360
 and the various techniques and how Caesar ciphers work.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:50.080
 And so we'll be back and talk about cryptographic hashes.

